{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd","id":834171,"title":"Realism and Stylism in WoD and CofD","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-20T18:22:21-08:00","id":834171,"title":"Realism and Stylism in WoD and CofD","content":"So recently in another thread, a point was made that some fans find certain aspects of WoD preferable to CofD because they are more realistic. This struck me as odd because that's one of the big reasons I and a lot of people I game with prefer CofD over WoD. I had always perceived WoD's appeal being based on its stylized alternate universe. The gothic punk aesthetic, the 90's attitude, the global supernatural conspiracies and epic, world-spanning meta-plots. Conversely, CofD's appeal, in my view, was based on its more subdued approach. The universe more closely resembles real life, the monsters hide in plain sight, the scale is (generally speaking) more personal rather than epic. I always saw WoD as a more developed alternate universe of its own, while CofD was more of a &quot;what if there were monsters in <i>our</i> universe?&quot; So this got me thinking about the role of realism and stylism in WoD and CofD - clearly both have a place in both settings, but they are utilized differently, and I am interested to read others' thoughts on the matter.<br /> <br /> Now, before we go any further, this discussion needs a disclaimer in a big way. This kind of discussion comparing WoD and CofD and peoples preferences for each has a tendency to lead to some serious flame wars. I'm aware that bringing up this topic is playing with fire, and I'm going to be keeping a close watch on this thread. If it starts to devolve into &quot;edition&quot; warring (for lack of a better term, since they're not actually editions of the same game, they're different IPs with similar subject matter), I won't hesitate to close it. Ways you can help avoid this are by phrasing things in clear terms of personal preference. &quot;I like this about WoD because...&quot; is fine, as is &quot;I didn't like this about CofD because...&quot; but &quot;WoD sucks because...&quot; or &quot;CofD is better because...&quot; are not. Respect the games and respect other people's preferences, and I think we will be able to have a mature discussion about setting elements in these two games.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T18:31:19-08:00","id":834175,"title":"","content":"It's odd, because both games have struck me as stylized in different ways-neither felt particularly rooted in realism. ChroD's approach might be construed as more mature and down to earth*, really quieter, but they neither has made a particular attempt to make realism a intended benefit or goal.<br /> <br /> And honestly, I'm not sure how much of an appeal to realism I would want in any game I played, given that the medium dwells in the abstract anyways.<br /> <br /> *Those might not be the right words, particularly mature, but I'm lacking better words right now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T19:03:36-08:00","id":834186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=834175#post834175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's odd, because both games have struck me as stylized in different ways-neither felt particularly rooted in realism. ChroD's approach might be construed as more mature and down to earth*, really quieter, but they neither has made a particular attempt to make realism a intended benefit or goal.<br /> <br /> And honestly, I'm not sure how much of an appeal to realism I would want in any game I played, given that the medium dwells in the abstract anyways.<br /> <br /> *Those might not be the right words, particularly mature, but I'm lacking better words right now.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, I think both have realistic and stylized elements. Perhaps realism isn't the best choice of words, but it has always seemed to me that CofD's style is subtler. Conversely, WoD has seemed more radical in the ways it deviates from reality. For example, the World of Darkness is quite explicitly darker than our world, in the sense that it's a less pleasant place. Whereas the world of the Chonicles of Darkness is, it's been said, &quot;darker&quot; in the sense that things are more hidden, less visible. I don't have much personal experience with WoD, but it's always appealed to me as its own distinct setting, whereas CofD I like because I can more easily imagine its supernatural inhabitants in my own world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T19:12:49-08:00","id":834189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd#post834171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So recently in another thread, a point was made that some fans find certain aspects of WoD preferable to CofD because they are more realistic. This struck me as odd because that's one of the big reasons I and a lot of people I game with prefer CofD over WoD. I had always perceived WoD's appeal being based on its stylized alternate universe. The gothic punk aesthetic, the 90's attitude, the global supernatural conspiracies and epic, world-spanning meta-plots. Conversely, CofD's appeal, in my view, was based on its more subdued approach. The universe more closely resembles real life, the monsters hide in plain sight, the scale is (generally speaking) more personal rather than epic. I always saw WoD as a more developed alternate universe of its own, while CofD was more of a &quot;what if there were monsters in <i>our</i> universe?&quot; So this got me thinking about the role of realism and stylism in WoD and CofD - clearly both have a place in both settings, but they are utilized differently, and I am interested to read others' thoughts on the matter.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. It's realy shows how different people get entirely different impressions from the same sources. Unlike you, I myself always felt the WoD more, not realistic, but more grounded exactly because the clans and traditions and whatnot firm base in real-world phenomenoms (even as stereotypes) and elaborate history. I get the appeal of playing clans as archetypical vampires without this, I just liked and preferred the WoD approach. I also am aware that the sub-splats, like bloodlines and later sourcebooks, not to mention the 2nd editions gave options for this, but still, CofD feeled to me as the supernaturals basicaly existing in their own world and the mundane world is just a background for this, while the WoD supernaturals are connected to the mundane world in myriad ways. They are part of history, sometimes shapers of history. I also feeled this way about the organizations in the game, the CofD ones feels that they are existing for their own sake (I get this feeling most in Awakening), while the WoD ones are the results of history and actual events. The Camarilla has history and reasons while it is as it is, while the Invictus didn't have as much. Moreso actualy the world-spanning conspiracies making more sense to me than the strict local style of CofD. I understand why people don't like these conspiracies and why the CofD approach appeals to STs who want to create their own settings instead of playing in someone's. But I think that supernaturals who connected to each other and flock into larger groups makes sense in the modern age, because of information, politics and other benefits. Like, again as the mundanes. I just having a hard time imagining that every city's supernatural scene existing in their own bubble with minimal or no connections to the larger world around them. And I think WoD can be played localy too, I just like the notion of a larger world around the characters with events and politics and caharacters, it feels to me more living.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T19:28:50-08:00","id":834191,"title":"","content":"As an afterthought, I like CofD too. If i want to do my own thing, or don't have the mood for the WoD's established world, CofD is perfect for that. It is also changes between gamelines what world I prefer. I like both Vampires, both Changelings, prefer Vigil over Reckoning, Ascension over Awakening. I slightly prefer Apocalypse over forsaken, but 2nd ed had closing the gap. I like Geist and Wraith never realy appealed to me. I immensely like that CofD have a REAL mundane line which is a thing on it's own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T19:45:29-08:00","id":834198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=834189#post834189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. It's realy shows how different people get entirely different impressions from the same sources. Unlike you, I myself always felt the WoD more, not realistic, but more grounded exactly because the clans and traditions and whatnot firm base in real-world phenomenoms (even as stereotypes) and elaborate history. I get the appeal of playing clans as archetypical vampires without this, I just liked and preferred the WoD approach.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess I can understand what you mean by that. For me, the reason archetypal Clans appeal to me more than Clans based on real-world groups is that I feel there's more room to deviate from archetypes. Also, some of the Masquerade Clans based on ethnic groups just didn't make sense to me - if anyone who's embraced by (for example) a Ravnos becomes a Ravnos no matter who they were in life, I would expect the Clan to be more racially diverse than they're usually presented. But I can see where you're coming from there, I guess the real-life inspirations could make things feel more grounded in human politics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=834189#post834189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also am aware that the sub-splats, like bloodlines and later sourcebooks, not to mention the 2nd editions gave options for this, but still, CofD feeled to me as the supernaturals basicaly existing in their own world and the mundane world is just a background for this, while the WoD supernaturals are connected to the mundane world in myriad ways. They are part of history, sometimes shapers of history. I also feeled this way about the organizations in the game, the CofD ones feels that they are existing for their own sake (I get this feeling most in Awakening), while the WoD ones are the results of history and actual events. The Camarilla has history and reasons while it is as it is, while the Invictus didn't have as much.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes me wonder if our different perspectives on the matter may come in part from when we were each introduced to CofD. Correct me if I'm wrong, but I would assume that you played WoD before CofD was a thing, and when the metaplot ended and Requiem came out as the first game in what was called &quot;the new World of Darkness&quot; at the time. Your impressions of CofD were likely formed by its early stages. I, on the other hand, hadn't even heard of either World of Darkness until the late 2000's (I don't remember exactly when, but I know Changeling the Lost was already a thing at the time), and didn't actually play until 2010. For the entire time I've been involved in the CofD fandom, the supplements detailing the background and histories of various supernatural organizations were already around. The Invictus (to continue your example) <i>does</i> have historical origin and reason for being, and has done since the first time I picked up a Requiem book.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=834189#post834189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Moreso actualy the world-spanning conspiracies making more sense to me than the strict local style of CofD. I understand why people don't like these conspiracies and why the CofD approach appeals to STs who want to create their own settings instead of playing in someone's. But I think that supernaturals who connected to each other and flock into larger groups makes sense in the modern age, because of information, politics and other benefits. Like, again as the mundanes. I just having a hard time imagining that every city's supernatural scene existing in their own bubble with minimal or no connections to the larger world around them. And I think WoD can be played localy too, I just like the notion of a larger world around the characters with events and politics and caharacters, it feels to me more living.</div> </div> </div> </div>I get that for sure. The idea of the epic scale of WoD is something I've always liked. But it's also part of what makes it strain my sense of verisimilitude more than the personal stories of CofD. Again, nothing wrong with that. I think it's cool as its own setting, but these vast global organizations that I know factually don't exist in the real world are much harder for me to suspend disbelief about than smaller, local organizations that I might just not have heard of. The thing about cities' supernatural populations living in isolated bubbles I could definitely see straining suspension of disbelief just as hard in the other direction, but again, that's something that was much more true of early CofD that the game has long since grown out of. You can especially see it in the Second Editions, but even before that there was a gradual movement towards more global interconnectivity in the supernatural communities. The stories CofD focuses on were still (and are still) generally more personal in scale rather than epic, but I prefer personal stories. Easier for me to identify with. (If it puts it into perspective, I'm one of those very, <i>very</i> few people who preferred Dragon Age 2 over Origins because I liked the personal story of Hawke over the epic story of the Warden).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T20:39:41-08:00","id":834231,"title":"","content":"I like the CofD for being a little bit more grounded, and for sticking to the &quot;these games are metaphors for serious things&quot; idea that Wraith championed so well. I take roleplaying very seriously as an artistic medium and love to see it mature into something capable of producing works like Changeling: the Lost, which really couldn't exist as any other form of media.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-20T20:55:11-08:00","id":834241,"title":"","content":"Good points. You get it right that I started with WoD (although more or less at the same time as you, a little bit earlier, few years before the closing of the old lines, but in Hungary WoD already had a fan-base, Vampire revised books in official translation in bookstores, novels and fan-translations of other lines). My base impressions of CofD are indeed formed mostly by the 1st ed corebooks. I also don't red much of the later sourcebooks, because of accessability and language barriers of that time (and later because of the lack of possibility to play, so lack of reason), but I'm aware that they are elaborated the history and connectedness aspects of the game and I flipped through most and red some (and it's worth noting that the corebooks of the WoD lines are mostly metaplot-light, so there isn't that huge the difference in my opinion). I did read Requiem 2nd ed and it is much &quot;better&quot; in that way indeed and i'm waiting curiously for the Covenants expansion for further elaboration. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I think you're right that suspension of disbelief works both ways regarding conspiracies and so, however for me, if we have formal and informal worldwide organizations in the real world I can imagine the same in the supnat scene. Also, the exact scale of these organizations is debatable. For example the Camarilla indeed have rules and Justicars and archons and inner circle, yes, but mostly the princes and local licks are doing their own things in their cities without interfering from above most of the time. Also, the Technocracy isn't realy that omnipotent and omniscient organization, because the lack of resources and infighting.<br /> <br /> As the epicness and local question. I think it's true that the bigger scope of WoD indeed tends to support epic chronicles and events, but there is room for local and personal stories. I think teh exact same opposite is true for the CofD. Honestly, in my eyes both can support both styles, but inclined differently. I also think the notion of epic-focusedness of WoD is mostly the result of metaplot and the novels/comicbooks/etc. which were focused on the epic stories of epic characters understandably, I think the largest part of games are mostly local-oriented. As a sidenote regarding novels: I liked the Three Shades of Night novel very much, I think it represented the strong sides of CofD, as local story(and history), local down-to-earth caharacters (okay, mostly) and crossover. I'm sad we didn't get any sequels and I'm still hoping for it, I liked those characters very much.<br /> <br /> Another thing is that WoD is more epic in the scope of powers most of the time (again 2nd ed closed the gap), so I think it strengthens the notion. And there is definitely more posturing, due to the gothic-punk vibe, but I like it, so it's a merit for me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Honestly, I'm very concerned about the last WW interview in which Martin said gothic-punk is dead. It isn't dead to me and honestly, I can't imagine the WoD without it. Gothic is from the mood and aesthetics (and requiem is also gothic) and punk is from the rebelling against the status quo and exactly against that big world-spanning conspiracies. I can't realy fathom a WoD without these. Or I can, but then it is CofD. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> But it's just me and my personal tastes. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The racial stereotypes clans are an interesting case. Most of the clans are cosmopolitan and the few that are not are more closed, more secular families or strict religious sects which is explains the phenomenon (and still them are embracing varied ethnicities in a varied quantity). In Requiem there would be (and in some cases are) bloodlines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-21-2016, 04:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T09:55:19-08:00","id":835127,"title":"","content":"I have covered most of my initial impressions on why WoD is more realistic than CofD in the interview thread. One more thing I would like to add is CofD's plot hooks. Boy, oh boy, this one's a backfiring paradox. Keep in mind that I noticed this when comparing CofD to V20 (might not be the case in WoD revised etc.), and also that I started with Requiem back in 2004 with no WoD experience whatsoever (so I am biased in that I've had too much of Requiem, so VtM is the exotic, shiny new thing to me).<br /> <br /> So, in CofD, your characters are most likely the focus of the entire chronicle. NPCs are bland, the tools allow for very powerful and interesting characters (both PCs and NPCs), and there's just so much potential unhindered by the overwhelming environment of WoD. Again, this was such a great idea it's a shame it doesn't work in practice. When Beckett's your Status 5 Cainite, you better be ready to up your roleplaying and make sure your character does something extraordinary at an already extraordinary and epic conflict that is Jyhad. Unfortunately, my experience with both LARP and tabletop Requiem means there's a lot of complacency. Average stories, about average characters, who just like it that way. Tell a bunch of players there's a Strix on the loose, chances are they'll pronounce it wrong (true story). Tell them that Caine, the mythical progenitor of all Cainites, is rumored to have awakened, and they'll type Book of Nod in drivethrurpg's online store faster than a Brujah with 5 dots of Celerity.<br /> <br /> So, the plot hooks are meant to fill in the blanks, give characters side quests in what seems to be one long side quest. Unfortunately, it takes a lot of effort on the ST's part (and more so for the players) to make the most out of Requiem. Which is such a pity I feel like we've failed the game (not vice versa). All I have is anecdotal, of course, but after taking the difficult decision of switching my ancient Athens chronicle to Masquerade, my suspicions were proven correct, at least for my troupe. I had been begging them to read the Requiem corebook, or at least their respective clan sections for 6 months. Only when I mentioned Masquerade and Gehenna, and explained what they were, did my players put some effort into it. They felt scared of the setting, and that they needed to prepare. Makes my job as ST easier... *insert evil laugh*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T11:08:25-08:00","id":835190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835127#post835127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have covered most of my initial impressions on why WoD is more realistic than CofD in the interview thread. One more thing I would like to add is CofD's plot hooks. Boy, oh boy, this one's a backfiring paradox. Keep in mind that I noticed this when comparing CofD to V20 (might not be the case in WoD revised etc.), and also that I started with Requiem back in 2004 with no WoD experience whatsoever (so I am biased in that I've had too much of Requiem, so VtM is the exotic, shiny new thing to me).<br /> <br /> So, in CofD, your characters are most likely the focus of the entire chronicle. NPCs are bland, the tools allow for very powerful and interesting characters (both PCs and NPCs), and there's just so much potential unhindered by the overwhelming environment of WoD. Again, this was such a great idea it's a shame it doesn't work in practice. When Beckett's your Status 5 Cainite, you better be ready to up your roleplaying and make sure your character does something extraordinary at an already extraordinary and epic conflict that is Jyhad. Unfortunately, my experience with both LARP and tabletop Requiem means there's a lot of complacency. Average stories, about average characters, who just like it that way. Tell a bunch of players there's a Strix on the loose, chances are they'll pronounce it wrong (true story). Tell them that Caine, the mythical progenitor of all Cainites, is rumored to have awakened, and they'll type Book of Nod in drivethrurpg's online store faster than a Brujah with 5 dots of Celerity.<br /> <br /> So, the plot hooks are meant to fill in the blanks, give characters side quests in what seems to be one long side quest. Unfortunately, it takes a lot of effort on the ST's part (and more so for the players) to make the most out of Requiem. Which is such a pity I feel like we've failed the game (not vice versa). All I have is anecdotal, of course, but after taking the difficult decision of switching my ancient Athens chronicle to Masquerade, my suspicions were proven correct, at least for my troupe. I had been begging them to read the Requiem corebook, or at least their respective clan sections for 6 months. Only when I mentioned Masquerade and Gehenna, and explained what they were, did my players put some effort into it. They felt scared of the setting, and that they needed to prepare. Makes my job as ST easier... *insert evil laugh*</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh, interesting. That's definitely very different from my own experience. I do see what you mean in that characters, NPCs, and plots in CofD are as good as the work the ST and the players put into them. But for me, that has been a positive thing. Also, I'm not sure how that relates to realism in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T13:25:49-08:00","id":835319,"title":"","content":"I think that the real problem between &quot;CWod&quot; and &quot;CotD&quot; was more a matter of style and writting than everything else.<br /> <br /> When &quot;NWoD&quot; came here in Spain, later as always, the translation was bad, but it wasn't exactly the translation that made the game flop, the translations of the &quot;OWoD&quot; books made by the editorial of that time was horrible too.<br /> It wasn't it, it was something else, something that is very easy to tell when you read it in english.<br /> <br /> It doesn't look like but my english lecture comprehension is better than my writting I asure you, you cannot belive that because probably this text is filled with errata but it's true, but I cannot show you that, I can only tell that fact to you.<br /> and that's was and id the big problem with &quot;NWod&quot;.<br /> The first edition of the books, it's doesnt matter on wich language, felt wrong because of the writing style.<br /> <br /> One of the best thing of the &quot;CWoD&quot; books it's that are partly written &quot;in universe&quot; basic and suplements alike. Let's take the &quot;Book of Worlds&quot; from Ascension and &quot;Astral Realms&quot; from Awakening, two mage books who mirrored each other very well - the two are the mage umbral book - for example.<br /> The most of the fluff and partly of the crunch of &quot;The book of Worlds&quot; was written &quot;in universe&quot; by various NPC, they narrated it to you: the different realms, the differents aspect of it, of it's inhabitants, the realms rules, a lot of inane things... everything, the narration doesn't show you nothing, it tell, tell you everithing you need to know and everything build the world, and it's fun to read, really really fun.<br /> It was close to the reader.<br /> <br /> On the other side &quot;Astral Realms&quot; was written as a textbook, the book doesnt show to you the thing it told, writen outside the world's game. That's isn't bad, most rpg books are written like that, the worst offender is the way it was written: everything have a pourpose, everything it's planed, there's no... there's no holes that can be filled with worldbuilding because everything need to have a reason to exist other than existing for the sake of existing for the flavor.<br /> The separation between crunch and fluff, the flavorless text, the &quot;theme&quot; and &quot;mood&quot; paragraphs... <br /> The book try to hard, completely screaming to the reader constantly &quot;I'm not Ascension, I'm not high!, winners don't do drugs, I'm gonna tell you everything you need to know but away of the fantasy I'm suposed to be.&quot;<br /> The book does't have a real personality and it's not so fun to read.<br /> <br /> That's doesn't mean that &quot;Astral Realms&quot; it's is a bad book, I really love it, Awakening it's lovely, I like it more than Ascension, the freedom it's gives to you, the possibility of striping completely of the metaplot even in it's &quot;I'm not Ascension, I have no metaplot&quot; form, yes Awakening, you have one, and you give the oportunity of not using it at all so I'm gonna take it, that's why I'm so cruel with it, and with Forsaken, and wih Requiem.<br /> <br /> I wish I was able to take the aproach of the &quot;CotD core rulebook&quot; vs &quot;CWoD core rulebook&quot; but I don't want to rekindle acient flamewars, that's as toxic as most of the rabbid vampire fanbase of Spain, it's so rabbid and is so toxic that they made me and my roleplaying companions out of the game forever, so not touching that, sorry and I really want to, because it's helps a lot in this matters.<br /> But I can talk to you instead about the Spain's flamewars of &quot;Requiem vs Masquerade&quot; for anthopological pouposes if you like.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nosolorol.com/nuevo/images/Fostern.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1192-aura","name":"Aura","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T13:43:34-08:00","id":835333,"title":"","content":"I started with WoD during the revised edition, by my teenage years, and my collection isn't that ample, unless I include some barely read PDFs. It was a phase to me which ended when they released the now aptly named CofD. Why? Because I had outgrown the rebellious goth-punkiness, eternal trench-coat katana wielding, doom and gloom games. Because the CofD gave the tools to play more mature games, in which I finally discovered immersion, because of it's personal horror focus.<br /> <br /> To me, comparing WoD with CofD is like comparing Burton/Schumacher's take on Batman VS Nolan's take on it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":576,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","user":"Ephsy","date":". 02-22-2016, 04:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T14:08:44-08:00","id":835358,"title":"","content":"Let's try to avoid judgement about the maturity of either game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T14:21:23-08:00","id":835370,"title":"","content":"The big difference to me between the two is there's a sense of a &quot;firewall&quot; between the supernatural and the mundane in WoD that doesn't seem to be there in CoD. CoD kinda feels like one way you could explain how our world could be so awful... because there are literal monsters out there causing bad things sometimes. Sure, sometimes it's people... but sometimes we tell ourselves they're ALL people because the truth is way worse. Because of this, every person matters. Every person is special and priceless if they die or are lost.<br /> <br /> By comparison, like... there are a bunch of world-spanning conspiracies in WoD, but it feels like they're a dime a dozen, and at the end of the day... this one negates that one and the overall results are null. People, including PCs, are mostly worthless and meaningless. It's not Lovecraftian, but it has a lot of the sense of pointlessness... in the end, no matter how mighty a mage or vampire you are... you're meaningless. All will crumble and fall, and it will happen sooner than later.<br /> <br /> To me that's the difference... the sense of EVERYTHING mattering to NOTHING mattering. And based on people who I knew who played, that was the reason they liked WoD... because they could play the worst most horrible characters and it didn't matter... maybe it says something about the WoD fans I've known, but...<br /> <br /> Note, I'm a fan of Wraith, and I enjoy WoD games... but this is the sense I get from the two settings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T14:28:01-08:00","id":835378,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835333#post835333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because I had outgrown the rebellious goth-punkiness, eternal trench-coat katana wielding, doom and gloom games. Because the CofD gave the tools to play more mature games, in which I finally discovered immersion, because of it's personal horror focus.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, but that's as much the players maturing as it is the games. Looking back at my first Requiem characters (or worse, my first chronicles as a storyteller for Requiem) I can tell you there were plenty of katanas to go around... In my defense I was 14. :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T14:33:35-08:00","id":835381,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835378#post835378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, but that's as much the players maturing as it is the games. Looking back at my first Requiem characters (or worse, my first chronicles as a storyteller for Requiem) I can tell you there were plenty of katanas to go around... In my defense I was 14. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>It probably got glossed over: To me, comparing WoD with CofD is like comparing Burton/Schumacher's take on Batman VS Nolan's take on it.<br /> <br /> Both takes were amazingly successful, but ultimately, a product of their time. When they release a 4th edition of WoD, I'm totally checking it out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T17:38:13-08:00","id":835479,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835370#post835370\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> By comparison, like... there are a bunch of world-spanning conspiracies in WoD, but it feels like they're a dime a dozen, and at the end of the day... this one negates that one and the overall results are null. People, including PCs, are mostly worthless and meaningless. It's not Lovecraftian, but it has a lot of the sense of pointlessness... in the end, no matter how mighty a mage or vampire you are... you're meaningless. All will crumble and fall, and it will happen sooner than later.<br /> <br /> To me that's the difference... the sense of EVERYTHING mattering to NOTHING mattering. And based on people who I knew who played, that was the reason they liked WoD... because they could play the worst most horrible characters and it didn't matter... maybe it says something about the WoD fans I've known, but...<br /> <br /> Note, I'm a fan of Wraith, and I enjoy WoD games... but this is the sense I get from the two settings.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The &quot;all is damned, we're all going to hell and nothing matters&quot; aspect is something I don't like ever and currently I'm very concerned about the upcoming 4th ed in this regard. I think the X20 editions, especialy Mage hit the sweat spot between the gonzoness and ancient horrors/conspiracies etc and overall mood that the characters and their acts are still matter.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T20:08:11-08:00","id":835544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835479#post835479\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The &quot;all is damned, we're all going to hell and nothing matters&quot; aspect is something I don't like ever and currently I'm very concerned about the upcoming 4th ed in this regard. I think the X20 editions, especialy Mage hit the sweat spot between the gonzoness and ancient horrors/conspiracies etc and overall mood that the characters and their acts are still matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a reason Wraith was my favorite... it was dark and grim, but there was a potential light at the end of the tunnel. Transcendance wasn't super common, but enough that a friend of a friend knew someone who supposedly had. It was the worst hidden secret of the Underworld.<br /> <br /> I don't mind a world where it's not stacked in my favor as long as I know that I CAN win... even if it's a low low chance. It makes it all the sweeter. (Win IC... winning OOC is having a fun time and cool story).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-23T02:08:08-08:00","id":835646,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835190#post835190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh, interesting. That's definitely very different from my own experience. I do see what you mean in that characters, NPCs, and plots in CofD are as good as the work the ST and the players put into them. But for me, that has been a positive thing. Also, I'm not sure how that relates to realism in the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Apologies. It's been hard forming coherent thoughts and expressing them appropriately these last few days, due to a regression in my mental health (despite going through steady therapy).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-23T02:24:01-08:00","id":835649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835646#post835646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apologies. It's been hard forming coherent thoughts and expressing them appropriately these last few days, due to a regression in my mental health (despite going through steady therapy).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, I think more than a few of us can understand that sort of thing. Take care, and it'll be here when you feel more capable. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-23T09:23:33-08:00","id":835829,"title":"","content":"I haven't had chance to play the rest NWOD (i hope that changes)so I have to limit this to VAMP.<br /> As someone who goes back to First Edition Masquerade, I prefer Requiem either old or New.<br /> <br /> Most of the 13 clans just don't make sense to me as clans. To me they are more suited as bloodlines in the requiem sense. Or as a covenant.<br /> If you are trying to get a masquerade player to try Requiem, You have to explain the 5 as meta-clans. Some closer to the old Lineages they used to build in Masquerade. You know how the Tzim and Ventrue and Maybe Brujah were of the Kingly lineage? While others were of the hunter Lineage?<br /> <br /> That is how you explain requiem clans to a masqer.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-24T15:31:22-08:00","id":836710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aura</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=835319#post835319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> One of the best thing of the &quot;CWoD&quot; books it's that are partly written &quot;in universe&quot; basic and suplements alike. Let's take the &quot;Book of Worlds&quot; from Ascension and &quot;Astral Realms&quot; from Awakening, two mage books who mirrored each other very well - the two are the mage umbral book - for example.<br /> The most of the fluff and partly of the crunch of &quot;The book of Worlds&quot; was written &quot;in universe&quot; by various NPC, they narrated it to you: the different realms, the differents aspect of it, of it's inhabitants, the realms rules, a lot of inane things... everything, the narration doesn't show you nothing, it tell, tell you everithing you need to know and everything build the world, and it's fun to read, really really fun.<br /> It was close to the reader.<br /> <br /> On the other side &quot;Astral Realms&quot; was written as a textbook, the book doesnt show to you the thing it told, writen outside the world's game. That's isn't bad, most rpg books are written like that, the worst offender is the way it was written: everything have a pourpose, everything it's planed, there's no... there's no holes that can be filled with worldbuilding because everything need to have a reason to exist other than existing for the sake of existing for the flavor.<br /> The separation between crunch and fluff, the flavorless text, the &quot;theme&quot; and &quot;mood&quot; paragraphs...<br /> The book try to hard, completely screaming to the reader constantly &quot;I'm not Ascension, I'm not high!, winners don't do drugs, I'm gonna tell you everything you need to know but away of the fantasy I'm suposed to be.&quot;<br /> The book does't have a real personality and it's not so fun to read.<br /> <br /> That's doesn't mean that &quot;Astral Realms&quot; it's is a bad book, I really love it, Awakening it's lovely, I like it more than Ascension, the freedom it's gives to you, the possibility of striping completely of the metaplot even in it's &quot;I'm not Ascension, I have no metaplot&quot; form, yes Awakening, you have one, and you give the oportunity of not using it at all so I'm gonna take it, that's why I'm so cruel with it, and with Forsaken, and wih Requiem.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I would second this notion. While I don't think the CofD supplemetns are bad books in any way, because no, they are not and I like every one I read. BUT. Because the approach is sandbox and the options in the books are indeed that, options, there's always the feeling that it's a gaming piece, what I read, which presents options and while these books indeed have in-character fiction, chapters, even whole books, the WoD's codified setting allows a more tangible in-character tone of writing and, again strenghtens the notion of a living world.<br /> <br /> Connected to this, the WoD presents real-world conflicts as the background conflicts of the games, like the ruling of 1% (the occupy elysium sthitck in anarchs unbound an lore of the clans are embodies this line perfectly, it's hilarious! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />), enviromentalism, the conflict between safety and freedom, etc. I personaly love the WoD exactly because of this. It inclines toward global scale stories, as metaplot (however, in actual play it's not mandatory), because these are global-scale (however also could be local) problems, but these are also the problems of our real world too, which makes the game more &quot;real&quot;, more connected, while the CofD's approach prefers local stories and deep, personal conflicts, the 2nd eds even more! WoD's conflicts might seemed to be &quot;forced&quot;, if someone don't want to focusing on &quot;real-life-gabbage&quot; in games, or don't interested in these conflicts at all, but in playing deep social connections, local conflicts and stuff, which in turn could make it feeling more real as Charlaquin pointed out. It realy depends on the person and the person's interests. I like both and can understand why anyone prefers one or the other. BUT, again, this approach makes CofD more &quot;abstract&quot;, more of an artificial setting, besides the former mentioned abstractivism of the splats, etc. <br /> <br /> I think the CofD can be very realistic as a setting, if the ST indeed put work into detailing the chronicle's phyisacl and historical background and NPCs and the game gives a million very good options to costumize, while the WoD also demands that work but provides a more real-life-connected setting on a global scale, but it is more calcified. It's not a bug, it's a feature in both cases.<br /> <br /> About maturity: I agree with stylanski (and heads up man, It's always a joy to read your well-thought-out writings in these boards! I could absolutely sympathize with your hard time, have problems too, just keep going! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ), that it's not because of one game is more mature than the other, rahter than the player. I can trenchcoat-catanad the CofD to hell and can play (and love to play) WoD with very deep phylosophical inclinations. Frankly, the one time I actualy seen a trenchcoat-catana in an actual woD product wan in Bloodlines with the hunter guy at the Holywood bar, and I can't help it, i laughed for minutes, because it was sooooo absurd and hilarious <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ). And I don't think the bigger scope of baseline conflicts, or the countercultural aspects (which again depends on gameline, faction and so on) makes it immature either (saying that is saying that only conformism is mature, which is, well, <i>an </i>opinion, just not my opinion). The manner of playing is what matters.<br /> <br /> Oh, and the &quot;90's&quot; thing... I honestly never understand that. I started WoD well in the mid-late 2000's and never felt it overly outdated and the X20 lines are absolutely up-to date to me. The base conflicts of the games are not &quot;90's&quot; stuff. Enviromentalism, global surveilance and the gap between social classes are very much the problems of nowadays (moreover in a bigger scope) too. I think, the notion comes from the personal changes in one's personality with time and which phase of life one's connecting a phenomenon subconsciously.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-24T17:25:42-08:00","id":836760,"title":"","content":"Something that always kept me away from the old World of Darkness was the sense that it was too confined. And something that will always keep me from being able to consider it realistic was its simplification of the world. Almost every problem has an easy-to-point at cause, usually some sort of supernatural. Vampire is notorious for this, where one can literally trace your lineage back to basically the beginning of humankind in thirteen easy steps. You can look through the books and read about certain characters and see why they did certain things. Whereas if you were to look at a history book you can read about certain real people and see that they did certain things, and if you dig deeper you can make some educated guesses particularly if you have access to their correspondences with others and personal viewpoints, but as you go farther and farther back in history it becomes pretty blurry, especially if you wander away from the more thoroughly studied civilizations that might have had records. But even then, most of it's going to be a mystery, tainted by the cynicism of the reader or the teller.<br /> <br /> This is why I thought old World of Darkness was too much of a 'story' to be considered realistic. It has a rich history, but it's like Lord of the Rings or Game of Thrones, everyone is accounted for in neat appendices, to the point that people might actually get angry if you start doing things not directly referenced in the books. Which is of course the point, it's not a bad thing, but you're part of a Storytelling collective. Your character can fight alongside Lucita de Aragorn or take direct orders from Sascha. And I think what the guy in control of White Wolf is doing is trying to revitalize that by getting the IP out there so more creative people can have more characters and events for you to play with.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons I liked new World of Darkness was that it felt more grand. There's an entire world out there and even the books can't cover it all. Vampire politics are different from one county to another, let alone city, state, country and continent. Since record-keeping is verboten and records destroyed by protectors and enemies alike, it always struck me as making more sense that there would be massive gaps in knowing exactly what's happened to the history of one splat. Even the metaplot, something I found to be a weak point in World of Darkness, was more appealing to me in new. I don't know every mekhet in the world, but I know Norris and grew to like him and the way he personified the shadowy spymaster stereotype of his clan before being diablerized by his friend and Prince. One of my favorite books, Belial's Brood, was the first non-fiction book to have a pretty detailed in-world narrative, with a character trying to find out the background of the group for typical vampire reasons and perhaps finding too much. And the vampire clan books, also in-game were specifically about a mysterious agent trying to find the history of their clans, and only finding deceit and further mystery, and not a character with an encyclopedic memory who will reliably tell you everything that happened to them since they were born.<br /> <br /> So, I certainly found new to be more realistic.<br /> <br /> The style difference, I wouldn't necessarily say it's 90s, but that's going to be far more of a personal thing to decide whether you like it or not. Aside from one game about being part of a larger narrative and the other about making your own, the classic game was much more about fighting an eternal struggle against unbeatable odds. Whether they were the Technocrats, the Wyrm, the Antediluvians, they were something that as a character you were meant to struggle against in a grey vs black showdown that would end in a typically gothic fashion. Make the most of the now, because tomorrow may never come. I really liked that they opened it up more as the books came along and especially in the 20th Anniversary editions but that's neither here nor there. Chronicles was much more about your character doing their own thing, and in some ways it can lack a lot of motivation, and place more of a workload on the player to find something for their character to want to do, or the storyteller to give them something to do and a reason to do it fast. In this way I suppose it's more like the open ended games of the post-d20 world like Eclipse Phase, or in video games a sandbox or MMO. I even build my stories emulating sandbox games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-24T21:24:50-08:00","id":836844,"title":"","content":"I'm very split on this as I think each has major aspects that embrace the (potential) reality of the situation and others that seem stylistic conventions.<br /> <br /> For examples:<br /> The x-splats (Clan, Auspice, Path, etc) of CoD seem more realistic than the various racial stereotypes of the WoD. Yes, they are archetypes, but it comes across as if they created the archetypes because they existed that way. The Lord of the Night vampires, the Fear of the Darkness werewolves, the Channel of Higher Power mages, the Unearthly Beauty prometheans, the Living Elemental changelings. Their existence formatted the archetypes and continues to cement them into the collective unconscious. Conversely, the y-splats (Covenants, Tribes, Orders) feel very forced and not as any kind of organic out-growth of the world these creatures would live in. While I can see many of those groups existing within those societies, they don't seem to be broadly encompassing enough to have enough influence to be the real driving points behind their lives. They make for fine religious-philosophical schools of thought and organizations to belong to, but they lack to broad scope to become major political influences. But this also keeps the major violent conflicts to a minimum. When you only have 15 vamps in your Covenant, you're not going to &quot;go to war&quot; against the other Covenant that has 16 vamps. 5 Packs of Uratha might get into it, but we're still talking less than 20 combatants on each side. That's going to make much less noise.<br /> <br /> The x-splats of the WoD (Clan, Tribe, Tradition, etc) seem to less realistic to me. All these very specific cultural and regional stereotypes seem too narrow to support large enough populations for them to be a driving force behind their lives. However, the y-splats (Cam/Sabbat/Anarchs, Garou/Wyrm/Fera, Council/Technocracy/Crafts) are large and opposing philosophical differences. Significant enough to create long running conflicts and major divisions among the societies while remaining broad enough to sustain large populations on each side. But then the on-going conflicts between these large groups seems to be where the realism breaks down. The all-out warfare discussed in various books seems to be something that would be very difficult to keep hidden for long, even with all the mind-manipulating powers available. The Concordia War is the one that really stood out for me. One story was going on about a battle between two factions on either side of a bridge. Even with much of it taking place in Chimerical reality, it would have looked like all-out gang warfare. But we're expected to believe that there were no authorities that interfered? No reporters that told the story? I know the Mists cover a lot, but even the mundane version of what happened would have been newsworthy. The WoD supernatural world is hidden, but it seems just barely and only by having a lot of in-place, automatic magical cover-up solutions (Delirium, Mists, Veil).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":375,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","user":"Papa Bear","date":". 02-25-2016, 12:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-28T13:56:06-08:00","id":838789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=836844#post836844\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The WoD supernatural world is hidden, but it seems just barely and only by having a lot of in-place, automatic magical cover-up solutions (Delirium, Mists, Veil).</div> </div> </div> </div>I will not defend some of the more extravagant events in WoD history. However, it depends on where you base your yardstick for realism. For me, realism begins and ends with the fact that the world presented in both lines is a work of fiction, in which supernatural creatures exist. CofD tries to maintain this sense of realism, by presenting a more or less uneventful hidden world. That's my major issue with the line. It is not realistic for a world <i>inhabited</i> by monsters.<br /> <br /> Other than that, I agree with you. It's just that, as I'm typing this, I'm reading about Masquerade's clans. These pages are rife with tragedy, emotion, horror, and concepts that enrich my real-world perspective, by causing me to head to wikipedia and read up on them. CofD simply does not provide that level of excitement. As I intend to avoid die-hard WoD fans, I can always change and fine tune the elements I incorporate in my stories. But the way monsters are presented in CofD sets up a very promising backdrop that fails to deliver. Like I said, uneventful and bland. Nothing any CofD character does can matter in the long run. That's where I feel the fourth wall coming tumbling down. Everything that's ever happened in its history is very <i>here and now</i>, with the exception of Requiem for Rome.<br /> <br /> As for the x-splats of WoD, I can see that in some clans, but I still think they encompass a more global view on traditions, cultural customs, and myths, then the vampires of Requiem which are bound by literary representations of vampires (the much praised archetypes). Also, I'd like to point out that Mage the Ascension's x-splats were spot on realistic (for those interested in the real-life occult). <br /> <br /> Sure, it's not bad, but there original intention under Achilli was to encompass archetypes found in Masquerade. Subgroups would offer a much needed variety (and I'm not just talking about bloodlines which became painstakingly narrow). So what you got, in a noir genre analogy, a grouping of femme fatales with the added option of playing femme fatales with an obsession with bees, the dead, and so on. Groups should have that, and so much more. The Malkavians are more than just lunatics, they are also seers, pranksters, psycho killers, share a hive mind, etc. I'm not saying these are all good, but they offer players a huge variety and a great selection of concepts.<br /> <br /> Personally, as I have said before, loving both CofD and WoD, I can't help but mix and match. In the end, I have my own ST'ing style and eventually plan to move on to a homebrewed game of supernatural horror and occult mystery that accommodates exactly the kind of stories I like to run. So it's probably not an issue with either worlds, but just a matter of personal taste and style.<br /> <br /> Can't wait to see how Requiem 2nd edition shapes up to be, as a lot of my (loving) criticism is aimed at 1e.<br /> VtM just happens to be the new and shiny thing for me (after 12 years of Requiem), and has a very immersive atmosphere to it.<br /> <br /> We'll see.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-28T17:16:16-08:00","id":838873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=838789#post838789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CofD simply does not provide that level of excitement. As I intend to avoid die-hard WoD fans, I can always change and fine tune the elements I incorporate in my stories. But the way monsters are presented in CofD sets up a very promising backdrop that fails to deliver. Like I said, uneventful and bland. Nothing any CofD character does can matter in the long run. That's where I feel the fourth wall coming tumbling down. Everything that's ever happened in its history is very <i>here and now</i>, with the exception of Requiem for Rome.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find the complaint that nothing a character can do in Chronicles matters in the long run, considering the exact complaints levied against World of Darkness, to be the very definition of irony. It's the same complaint some people have of oWoD and not being able to do anything with games because the metaplot will supersede anything that you do.<br /> <br /> The truth is, if this is a problem, it is one you're bringing to the table. It is not supported by the books. There are over a hundred different bloodlines written up in the books, some having been created as recently as the 90s, some so new and unique that they haven't spread beyond their progenitor. Any character you create can be the founder of a new bloodline. Or covenant. There are also covenants that are new and widespread. In werewolf, Protectorates and Lodges rise and fall, there's even potential for new tribes.<br /> <br /> The only thing keeping you from making long lasting changes in your games is you and your Storyteller.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 02-28-2016, 10:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-29T01:53:34-08:00","id":839028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=838789#post838789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I will not defend some of the more extravagant events in WoD history. However, it depends on where you base your yardstick for realism. For me, realism begins and ends with the fact that the world presented in both lines is a work of fiction, in which supernatural creatures exist. CofD tries to maintain this sense of realism, by presenting a more or less uneventful hidden world. That's my major issue with the line. It is not realistic for a world <i>inhabited</i> by monsters.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that's somehow more realistic than pulling all the vicious supernatural crap say, vampires can pull (and have, check out Sabbat and Camarilla open conflicts), and somehow keep a huge human population in the dark... in the 21st century? Please.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=838789#post838789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Other than that, I agree with you. It's just that, as I'm typing this, I'm reading about Masquerade's clans. These pages are rife with tragedy, emotion, horror, and concepts that enrich my real-world perspective, by causing me to head to wikipedia and read up on them. CofD simply does not provide that level of excitement. As I intend to avoid die-hard WoD fans, I can always change and fine tune the elements I incorporate in my stories. But the way monsters are presented in CofD sets up a very promising backdrop that fails to deliver. Like I said, uneventful and bland. Nothing any CofD character does can matter in the long run. That's where I feel the fourth wall coming tumbling down. Everything that's ever happened in its history is very <i>here and now</i>, with the exception of Requiem for Rome.</div> </div> </div> </div>It also contains basically Gypsy vampires. <i>Gypsy. Vampires.</i> Excitement isn't what's being discussed here (nevermind how subjective it is.)<br /> <br /> Your setting in WoD? it's what your ST makes of it. And I could say, nothing, absolutely nothing any character does in the WoD matters in the face of the metaplot. To put things into perspective: My character managed to keep a truce between forsaken and pure. How? If they didn't, a huge tidal wave would fuck up their shit.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-29T07:49:59-08:00","id":839170,"title":"","content":"I feel like the CoD games I've been in have had far more effect on the history of the world than any game I ever played in WoD. And I've played a LOT more WoD than CoD. The metaplot thing was kinda always there and it seemed to encourage some... bad GMing habits, like the GM using an NPC to show how awesome they are or as a stick to force what they want. Now, I'm not saying all GMs, but it was one of those things I noticed.<br /> <br /> In CoD there's... a lot of openness for PCs to make their mark on the world. There is so much history, yet, always room to make more. Will you be someone who creates an idea or power or organization to help shape the world? I always felt like WoD for the most part... all that stuff was either A) already done or B) it's horrifying you create something new, so you and it must be wiped out. Nothing new is good.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-01T02:20:53-08:00","id":839757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=839028#post839028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also contains basically Gypsy vampires. <i>Gypsy. Vampires.</i> Excitement isn't what's being discussed here (nevermind how subjective it is.)<br /> <br /> Your setting in WoD? it's what your ST makes of it. And I could say, nothing, absolutely nothing any character does in the WoD matters in the face of the metaplot. To put things into perspective: My character managed to keep a truce between forsaken and pure. How? If they didn't, a huge tidal wave would fuck up their shit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Who are, if I read their Lore of the Clans section correctly, one of the most noble and misunderstood clan in VtM though.<br /> <br /> Perhaps I should be thankful that I first got into Requiem. I am in the process of starting a chronicle in which the protagonists will play through the ages trying to bring down the Aralu by uniting portions of the clans across sects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-01T02:23:39-08:00","id":839761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=838873#post838873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The truth is, if this is a problem, it is one you're bringing to the table. It is not supported by the books. There are over a hundred different bloodlines written up in the books, some having been created as recently as the 90s, some so new and unique that they haven't spread beyond their progenitor. Any character you create can be the founder of a new bloodline. Or covenant. There are also covenants that are new and widespread. In werewolf, Protectorates and Lodges rise and fall, there's even potential for new tribes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, in my defense, most of Requiem's bloodlines are bland and have had little interaction with their environments. Which, for the small population that is the entire Kindred populace across the globe, it's pretty unrealistic. Not all of them demonstrated the fluff and story potential of the Icarian Heresy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-01T06:26:51-08:00","id":839850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=839761#post839761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, in my defense, most of Requiem's bloodlines are bland and have had little interaction with their environments. Which, for the small population that is the entire Kindred populace across the globe, it's pretty unrealistic. Not all of them demonstrated the fluff and story potential of the Icarian Heresy.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're expressing opinion as fact again. Not all vampires are Alexander the Great or Caine. Just as the amount of actual people who have made tremendous impacts in the world is small, the amount of vampires who have made tremendous impacts in the world is small. This is what I consider realistic. And how little interaction they've had with their environment really doesn't matter, a new bloodline is a new bloodline and is practically an entire new species of vampire. And I don't believe a bloodline that is, 'Just like that clan, but they're on the Sabbat/Camarilla/Anarchs,' is any less bland.<br /> <br /> Just because they haven't devoted entire books to single bloodlines does not mean that they have any less potential than something in Masquerade. In fact, as Masquerade has already boxed in much of the history of bloodlines, it means there's less potential to be had for them there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-02T09:46:51-08:00","id":840726,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=839850#post839850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're expressing opinion as fact again. Not all vampires are Alexander the Great or Caine. Just as the amount of actual people who have made tremendous impacts in the world is small, the amount of vampires who have made tremendous impacts in the world is small. This is what I consider realistic. And how little interaction they've had with their environment really doesn't matter, a new bloodline is a new bloodline and is practically an entire new species of vampire. And I don't believe a bloodline that is, 'Just like that clan, but they're on the Sabbat/Camarilla/Anarchs,' is any less bland.<br /> <br /> Just because they haven't devoted entire books to single bloodlines does not mean that they have any less potential than something in Masquerade. In fact, as Masquerade has already boxed in much of the history of bloodlines, it means there's less potential to be had for them there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was actually referring to the really niche bloodlines of early 1st ed. Given that vampires operate akin to secret societies, and how the leading figures of said secret societies tend to do things that receive notice and attention by even the lowliest ranks (see how much controversy surrounded the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn for example), I would expect something close to that. Simply because I never got on board the &quot;vampires are just like us, only with fangs and powers&quot;.<br /> <br /> Also, yes, I would like more stable, recurring mentions for bloodlines (which was done beautifully for, say, the Khaibit and Malkavians). These are all opinions though, based on my sense of what feels realistic or not. Sorry if it seemed otherwise.<br /> <br /> Finally, and this will eventually go into my forum signature, though I have temporarily switched to Masquerade for some of that sweet, globe-trotting action, my style of play and storytelling is deeply influenced by CofD. To such an extent, in fact, that WoD old-timers don't enjoy playing in my chronicles because &quot;I don't respect the setting and metaplot&quot;. For this, I am thankful to CofD. It really allowed for a unique, almost artistic expression, on the ST's behalf, that would've been impossible had CofD not been introduced.<br /> <br /> One last thing. I was reading about Mesopotamian religion and I have to admit that Requiem is on spot about the origins of Vampires and the overall thematic background. I felt the Babylonian material in Ancient Mysteries was lacking here and there, but it hit the nail by not going with a Judeo-Christian mythos.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 03-02-2016, 12:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-02T11:51:46-08:00","id":840808,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=840726#post840726\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was actually referring to the really niche bloodlines of early 1st ed. Given that vampires operate akin to secret societies, and how the leading figures of said secret societies tend to do things that receive notice and attention by even the lowliest ranks (see how much controversy surrounded the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn for example), I would expect something close to that. Simply because I never got on board the &quot;vampires are just like us, only with fangs and powers&quot;.<br /> <br /> Also, yes, I would like more stable, recurring mentions for bloodlines (which was done beautifully for, say, the Khaibit and Malkavians). These are all opinions though, based on my sense of what feels realistic or not. Sorry if it seemed otherwise.<br /> <br /> Finally, and this will eventually go into my forum signature, though I have temporarily switched to Masquerade for some of that sweet, globe-trotting action, my style of play and storytelling is deeply influenced by CofD. To such an extent, in fact, that WoD old-timers don't enjoy playing in my chronicles because &quot;I don't respect the setting and metaplot&quot;. For this, I am thankful to CofD. It really allowed for a unique, almost artistic expression, on the ST's behalf, that would've been impossible had CofD not been introduced.<br /> <br /> One last thing. I was reading about Mesopotamian religion and I have to admit that Requiem is on spot about the origins of Vampires and the overall thematic background. I felt the Babylonian material in Ancient Mysteries was lacking here and there, but it hit the nail by not going with a Judeo-Christian mythos.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So I was reading this and I'm not sure that you're 100% right about that style requiring CoD... but what I'm saying here might be based more on headcanon/bias... I loved Wraith. Hands down, my favorite WoD game. The thing is, I always felt like it was the one least tied to the metaplot. Like there WAS one (I felt it was because the others had metaplots, so it was hard to escape for Wraith), but it was something you could just as easily ignore. Wraith felt more like a CoD game made years before CoD came out.<br /> <br /> I'm curious on what anyone else thinks of this theory/concept.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-03T16:19:38-08:00","id":841559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=840808#post840808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I was reading this and I'm not sure that you're 100% right about that style requiring CoD... but what I'm saying here might be based more on headcanon/bias... I loved Wraith. Hands down, my favorite WoD game. The thing is, I always felt like it was the one least tied to the metaplot. Like there WAS one (I felt it was because the others had metaplots, so it was hard to escape for Wraith), but it was something you could just as easily ignore. Wraith felt more like a CoD game made years before CoD came out.<br /> <br /> I'm curious on what anyone else thinks of this theory/concept.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've never looked into Wraith before the Kickstarter, and I'm waiting for the book to really delve into it.<br /> <br /> But from what little I do know I could see why you'd feel that way. It might seem least tied to the metaplot because it is, literally, least tied to the metaplot. For the most part, it takes place in another world. While things in the Skinlands could affect things in the Shadowlands, most of the metaplot happenings didn't really cross. There's obvious exceptions, but they're not as common as in the Skinlands, where you can just stumble into them.<br /> <br /> Another thing I thought makes it feel more like a premature Chronicles game would be the emphasis on archetypes, specifically with the arcanoi and their representing the various abilities of ghosts you see in ghost stories, games, and other media.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-03T17:03:24-08:00","id":841586,"title":"","content":"As someone who owned everything but the dice from Wraith (and still do, even my first ed glows in the dark still), I think you hit that point very much on the nose. Arcanoi weren't just a power by presentation, but they said something about your character... how they interacted with the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-06T18:37:24-08:00","id":843230,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure that I'd use &quot;realism&quot; as the defining factor in either game, but they're definitely more distinct.<br /> <br /> I'd say CoD is closer to our universe insofar as they draw more on mundane experience and wear their metaphors closer to their chest. When you play Requiem, your experience (at least in the early to mid-game) is a fair reflection of your own reality: a nobody living at the mercy of self-interested pricks who have more power than you. Lost is about coping with trauma. Awakening seems much less focused on this eternal global battle and more about the personal thirst for knowledge. All of these games seem to exist in a world that's pretty similar to our own because there's not a massive mythology in place and because the experience tends to be much more grounded. They don't give you a whole lot of expanded mythology to work with, so any ST is going to be drawing more on personal and real world experience to flesh out their settings.<br /> <br /> WoD always seemed more like a flat-out fantasy setting to me. There's so much mythology built out as to seem overwhelming, which is great for providing an ST with more options and great for a high-flying urban fantasy game. There's so much there and it's all so quantified, from the spelled-out mysteries behind the myths to the power levels that hit the double digits. There's nothing wrong with that by any means, but it creates a less thematically focused game and one that's more withdrawn from our world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-06T20:45:29-08:00","id":843279,"title":"","content":"I get where you're came from and there is truth in that. However, I'm still thinking that WoD, as a setting is more <i>anchored</i> to our own reality with million threads, because these fleshed-out mythologies and splats have real-world connections to real-world mythologies, subcultures, pop-culture, history, events and so. CofD is more distinct and abstract for me. For example, the kiths in CtD are from actual legends and fairy tales, when the CtL ones are archetypes. I feel I had a lot more connection to Ascension's traditions than to Awakening's paths and Orders, because I can relate to the Hermetic order, or to the druidic Verbenae, or to the Akashayana, much more than to the Obrimos, Acanthus or Adamantine Arrow (I'm pretty much looking forward to how Awakening 2nd edition will handle this 'problem', the open dev excerpst semms promising!). DtF's demons are actual fallen angels with Lucifer an co, when DtD's are the creations of the God-Machine, a wholy in-universe phenomenon with no real-world origin. Don't get me wrong, I like the concept and the story, but it is it's own universe. Aside from this, I agree that CofD's mundane side of the setting is closer to our reality as the supernatural don't have as much power over the mundane world (and the setting as a whole lacks the distinct gothic-punk theme). It's focus is much more the down-to-ground life of the characters and I understand why one could feel more connected to this style and immersed more easily in the setting. So, from a character's viewpoint, yes, it's seems more like our reality on the surface. It <i>could </i>resemble the real world as much as the WoD <i>if </i>the ST takes her time and incorporate real-world elemnts, like local legends, history, gorups etc. but as written, the supernatural side of the game is more distinct, to me, than WoD's, more like it is it's own universe, less the derivative of our own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 03-07-2016, 12:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-06T20:55:11-08:00","id":843285,"title":"","content":"I think the crux of the issue is, in WoD STs have most of the setting work cut out for them. With dozens of books telling you how stuff is in several settings, and what's transpired on them, it falls squarely into the CofD's ST to bring such elements to life. And it's something that's been remedied largely in second edition with example territories being covered in the corebooks, likely to be expanded upon in future releases. The disadvantage to such an approach was of course that later editions actualized their content without possibility to account for the exploits of PCs in those settings, giving birth to the so characterized metaplot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T07:53:59-08:00","id":843433,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=839761#post839761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, in my defense, most of Requiem's bloodlines are bland and have had little interaction with their environments. Which, for the small population that is the entire Kindred populace across the globe, it's pretty unrealistic. Not all of them demonstrated the fluff and story potential of the Icarian Heresy.</div> </div> </div> </div>You think the Gethsemini are bland? Or the Children of The Morrigan? <br /> In fact in Second Edition Bloodlines are more common. They are refinements of the gestalts of the clans.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T08:51:45-08:00","id":843455,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843279#post843279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get where you're came from and there is truth in that. However, I'm still thinking that WoD, as a setting is more <i>anchored</i> to our own reality with million threads, because these fleshed-out mythologies and splats have real-world connections to real-world mythologies, subcultures, pop-culture, history, events and so. CofD is more distinct and abstract for me. For example, the kiths in CtD are from actual legends and fairy tales, when the CtL ones are archetypes. I feel I had a lot more connection to Ascension's traditions than to Awakening's paths and Orders, because I can relate to the Hermetic order, or to the druidic Verbenae, or to the Akashayana, much more than to the Obrimos, Acanthus or Adamantine Arrow (I'm pretty much looking forward to how Awakening 2nd edition will handle this 'problem', the open dev excerpst semms promising!). DtF's demons are actual fallen angels with Lucifer an co, when DtD's are the creations of the God-Machine, a wholy in-universe phenomenon with no real-world origin. Don't get me wrong, I like the concept and the story, but it is it's own universe. Aside from this, I agree that CofD's mundane side of the setting is closer to our reality as the supernatural don't have as much power over the mundane world (and the setting as a whole lacks the distinct gothic-punk theme). It's focus is much more the down-to-ground life of the characters and I understand why one could feel more connected to this style and immersed more easily in the setting. So, from a character's viewpoint, yes, it's seems more like our reality on the surface. It <i>could </i>resemble the real world as much as the WoD <i>if </i>the ST takes her time and incorporate real-world elemnts, like local legends, history, gorups etc. but as written, the supernatural side of the game is more distinct, to me, than WoD's, more like it is it's own universe, less the derivative of our own.</div> </div> </div> </div>This seems to be a recurring theme in the discussion. We've got the CofD camp saying the world of CofD seems more down-to-earth without the fictional mythologies and conspiracies. And the WoD camp saying the characters of WoD seem more connected with real-life myths and groups. This definitely explains the root of the disagreement about which is more &quot;realistic&quot; (I use quotation marks there because as we've established, realism isn't really the issue here, but I'm not sure what other word to use.) I think I have a better understanding of the other perspective on this now. Thank you for this post!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T09:11:39-08:00","id":843466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843455#post843455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This seems to be a recurring theme in the discussion. We've got the CofD camp saying the world of CofD seems more down-to-earth without the fictional mythologies and conspiracies. And the WoD camp saying the characters of WoD seem more connected with real-life myths and groups. This definitely explains the root of the disagreement about which is more &quot;realistic&quot; (I use quotation marks there because as we've established, realism isn't really the issue here, but I'm not sure what other word to use.) I think I have a better understanding of the other perspective on this now. Thank you for this post!</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's go with verisimilitude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T09:14:50-08:00","id":843468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843466#post843466\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's go with verisimilitude.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good word for it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T09:58:50-08:00","id":843498,"title":"","content":"Another recurring notion, that came up in the various threads on various forums relating WWP's ME's latest two interviews, that is from the birth of NWoD until today, it has been struggling to gain foothold outside US, when it became there nearly as big as the WoD, with a passionate fandom, while in other parts of the world and mainly in Europe, the CWoD remained prevalent. I don't want to open up a big can of worms full of bad stereotypes. It is, however an interesting phenomenon for me and I don't think it is only because language barriers (however the WoD's being translated to many languages certainly helped!), since a lot of english-only rpg's are popular in many countries. Maybe, just maybe it also helped that the CWoD (while it was mainly US-centric) has history and splats which are rooted in old-world history and mythologies, thus offers more points to connect to people from those regions, while CofD is more 'here-and-now' with a much lesser real-world connected mythologies and more abstract splats, which is... appailing isn't realy a good word, but much less unattractive as a quality for someone grown up in the US. Again, I don't want to go into bad cultural stereotypes and I'm avare that later supplements have been closing this gap, but many of us gained their base impressions from the first ed NwoD corebooks. 2nd edition is much better as we stated many times, but I still didn't gain the same connectedness as an european. I'm very much looking forward to Dark Eras it seems a quite fine in this regard! Requiem for Regina and Lily, sabre and thorn (although I don't realy get hy it is 'sabre' instead of rapier, or blade...) are my most anticipated ones, because the 16-17th centuries are among my favourite periods due to my europen martial arts and historical interest. And Brothers grimm, because of, well, classics are classics! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Also I'm eager to read Budapest's writing in CtL 2e because I'm a hungarian and the Carpathian region has soooo many fairy-related myths (beside vampiric ones and, well, every other critters, but it is not very common knowledge nowadays, sadly).<br /> <br /> A last sidenote: I read CofD 2e corebook's horrors section lately and it was an excellent section (realy, the whole book was super), very usable and a good glimpse into urban legends in the US. I just wish the writers would be incorporated one or two from other regions too!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 03-07-2016, 01:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T10:53:39-08:00","id":843518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843498#post843498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another recurring notion, that came up in the various threads on various forums relating WWP's ME's latest two interviews, that is from the birth of NWoD until today, it has been struggling to gain foothold outside US, when it became there nearly as big as the WoD, with a passionate fandom, while in other parts of the world and mainly in Europe, the CWoD remained prevalent. I don't want to open up a big can of worms full of bad stereotypes. It is, however an interesting phenomenon for me and I don't think it is only because language barriers (however the WoD's being translated to many languages certainly helped!), since a lot of english-only rpg's are popular in many countries. Maybe, just maybe it also helped that the CWoD (while it was mainly US-centric) has history and splats which are rooted in old-world history and mythologies, thus offers more points to connect to people from those regions, while CofD is more 'here-and-now' with a much lesser real-world connected mythologies and more abstract splats, which is... appailing isn't realy a good word, but much less unattractive as a quality for someone grown up in the US. Again, I don't want to go into bad cultural stereotypes and I'm avare that later supplements have been closing this gap, but many of us gained their base impressions from the first ed NwoD corebooks. 2nd edition is much better as we stated many times, but I still didn't gain the same connectedness as an european. I'm very much looking forward to Dark Eras it seems a quite fine in this regard! Requiem for Regina and Lily, sabre and thorn (although I don't realy get hy it is 'sabre' instead of rapier, or blade...) are my most anticipated ones, because the 16-17th centuries are among my favourite periods due to my europen martial arts and historical interest. And Brothers grimm, because of, well, classics are classics! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Also I'm eager to read Budapest's writing in CtL 2e because I'm a hungarian and the Carpathian region has soooo many fairy-related myths (beside vampiric ones and, well, every other critters, but it is not very common knowledge nowadays, sadly).</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't doubt there's something to that. 2nd Edition CofD is definitely addressing a lot of those issues, and moving away from being so USA-centric, but you only get one chance to make a first impression. Despite the improvements 2e has made, it's a sad fact that outside the US it seems much harder to find groups to play CofD when most would rather play WoD.<br /> <br /> RE: Sabre vs. rapier, sabre is a type of fencing sword","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T11:11:40-08:00","id":843527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843518#post843518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> RE: Sabre vs. rapier, sabre is a type of fencing sword</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I know, but sabres are came into use in Western Europe around a century later. In the 17th century the most widespread form of sword in Eurpoe was the rapier (hence, the 3 musketeers). Sabre is more 18th century-Napoleonic-to-Victorian periods and it became as one of the olympic weapons because it was a military weapon first. I think, mostly the title sounds better this way than with the other options and that's it! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> But I don't want to go more off-topic. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I realy-realy hope Budapest's gonna be great in CtL 2e. I have some friends here who are not realy played any rpgs until this and were interested in the concept of CtL when I talked about it, so it would strengthen my motivation and help their immersion.<br /> <br /> Are we know anything about the city descriptions in MtAw 2e?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T11:38:31-08:00","id":843538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843433#post843433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You think the Gethsemini are bland? Or the Children of The Morrigan?<br /> In fact in Second Edition Bloodlines are more common. They are refinements of the gestalts of the clans.<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Ehhh, the Gethsemani show potential (though these two bloodlines are way too niche for my taste). I don't want more bloodlines though. I want a few <i>core ones</i>, that are presented in more depth and have a wider interaction within the Danse Macabre. A book in the style of the clanbooks that investigates the Sangiovanni and some other favorites? My hands will throw money at the screen faster than my computer can digitize and transfer them to OPP's bank account.<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, look at <a href=\"https://wodindex.wikispaces.com/Bloodlines\" target=\"_blank\">this list</a>. How many more bloodlines do we need anyway?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 03-07-2016, 02:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T11:55:37-08:00","id":843542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843538#post843538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ehhh, the Gethsemani show potential (though these two bloodlines are way too niche for my taste). I don't want more bloodlines though. I want a few <i>core ones</i>, that are presented in more depth and have a wider interaction within the Danse Macabre. A book in the style of the clanbooks that investigates the Sangiovanni and some other favorites? My hands will throw money at the screen faster than my computer can digitize and transfer them to OPP's bank account.<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, look at <a href=\"https://wodindex.wikispaces.com/Bloodlines\" target=\"_blank\">this list</a>. How many more bloodlines do we need anyway?</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm, I think i would do that too! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It is somewhat a dichotomy: Requiem clans are 'too generic' to have Masquearde-like interactuions, bloodlines, instead are 'too minutie'. But would it be good to have bloodlines be more interactive? Doesn't it would take Requiem closer to Masquerade in the sense of conspiracies and metaplot which many of the CofD fans who prefer the sandbox approach would resent? Where would be the fine line? Just playing the devil's advocate here. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T12:31:27-08:00","id":843564,"title":"","content":"On the subject of Bloodlines, one of the things I loved about making the switch to 2e was the obsolescence of all the 1e bloodlines, to be honest. It cut down on the bloat and brought Requiem back to its roots, and if I really want to bring some of my favorite bloodlines over, I can use homebrew. But because of the extra work that takes, it becomes something done sparingly. You ask yourself &quot;should I play a Bloodline?&quot; instead of &quot;which Bloodline should I play?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T12:37:36-08:00","id":843569,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843542#post843542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm, I think i would do that too! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It is somewhat a dichotomy: Requiem clans are 'too generic' to have Masquearde-like interactuions, bloodlines, instead are 'too minutie'. But would it be good to have bloodlines be more interactive? Doesn't it would take Requiem closer to Masquerade in the sense of conspiracies and metaplot which many of the CofD fans who prefer the sandbox approach would resent? Where would be the fine line? Just playing the devil's advocate here. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I've spoken before of Metamphiesophobia, and how detrimental it can be to Requiem's own identity. I'd love more meta, more plot, but no metaplot.<br /> <br /> Meta- refers to the self-referencing part. Requiem does that already, and it should do it more often (personal opinion, as always).<br /> -plot refers to a plot, a setting, and we already have a default setting: The Strix Chronicles. Now it's time to focus all that 1e material in relation to a constant of history. Why? Because vampires are too few not to interact. Because a rich setting in a game of immortal monsters is awesome and the way the game is designed, you could just discard it. It's one thing to say that Santino Sangiovanni did 1, 2, 3, and a whole other thing to say that, starting from book x,y,z, Santino renounced his membership in the Lancea and joined the Invictus (thus making non-Invictus Sangiovanni personae non grata in the family, and Theban Sorcery is now only accessible to older Necromancers).<br /> <br /> Metaplot, on the other hand, is &quot;the overarching storyline that binds together events in the official continuity of a published role-playing game campaign setting. Major official story events that change the world, or simply move important non-player characters from one place to another, are part of the metaplot for a game.&quot;. That's really hard to do just by adding a richer history and culture to bloodlines.<br /> <br /> Also, Charlaquin, I wholeheartedly agree with you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T13:09:24-08:00","id":843586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843569#post843569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've spoken before of Metamphiesophobia, and how detrimental it can be to Requiem's own identity. I'd love more meta, more plot, but no metaplot.<br /> <br /> Meta- refers to the self-referencing part. Requiem does that already, and it should do it more often (personal opinion, as always).<br /> -plot refers to a plot, a setting, and we already have a default setting: The Strix Chronicles. Now it's time to focus all that 1e material in relation to a constant of history. Why? Because vampires are too few not to interact. Because a rich setting in a game of immortal monsters is awesome and the way the game is designed, you could just discard it. It's one thing to say that Santino Sangiovanni did 1, 2, 3, and a whole other thing to say that, starting from book x,y,z, Santino renounced his membership in the Lancea and joined the Invictus (thus making non-Invictus Sangiovanni personae non grata in the family, and Theban Sorcery is now only accessible to older Necromancers).<br /> <br /> Metaplot, on the other hand, is &amp;quot;the overarching storyline that binds together events in the official continuity of a published role-playing game campaign setting. Major official story events that change the world, or simply move important non-player characters from one place to another, are part of the metaplot for a game.&amp;quot;. That's really hard to do just by adding a richer history and culture to bloodlines.<br /> <br /> Also, Charlaquin, I wholeheartedly agree with you.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're on the same page as the developers here. 2e has been much less gunshy about establishing definite background and history for the settings than 1e was. There is still a lot left open and sandboxy, and there's still no meta-plot (both good things in my opinion) but it is establishing canonical historical events, like some of the stuff we're seeing in Dark Eras, and the example Domains and territories. I've been loving seeing the Tokyo crossover setting develop over time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T13:13:26-08:00","id":843588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/834171-realism-and-stylism-in-wod-and-cofd?p=843542#post843542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm, I think i would do that too! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It is somewhat a dichotomy: Requiem clans are 'too generic' to have Masquearde-like interactuions, bloodlines, instead are 'too minutie'. But would it be good to have bloodlines be more interactive? Doesn't it would take Requiem closer to Masquerade in the sense of conspiracies and metaplot which many of the CofD fans who prefer the sandbox approach would resent? Where would be the fine line? Just playing the devil's advocate here. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The 2e core book actually presents several examples of clans developing distinct cultures and identities and interacting with other clans as clans. It's just more local. There's even a city that's basically run by a primogen council, but without a prince.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-07T13:47:53-08:00","id":843609,"title":"","content":"I honestly don't have a problem with the huge number of bloodlines given how the setting is built. Everything is so localized that you're unlikely to have more than a few specific bloodlines in a given city, and they don't come packed with a bunch of metaplot bloat.<br /> <br /> I think it also adds an interesting emphasis to the whole strange and unpredictable nature of vampiric existence to know that new strains of vampirism can just spring into existence with little fanfare.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-08T14:23:49-08:00","id":844148,"title":"","content":"Though one of the bloodlines I absolutely love are the Agonistes. Hope we see more of those guys...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-28T15:45:10-07:00","id":854699,"title":"","content":"So... I've, err... Been reading the news lately. Vampire overlords pulling the strings of humanity in an endless jyhad, unfortunately, wins out in realism.<br /> <br /> Fuck.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"h